WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.832 --> 00:00:04.290
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Juggling is hard.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.400
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
this is even harder

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.020
than setting up
logging and monitoring

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.535
for my cloud infrastructure.

00:00:08.535 --> 00:00:09.990
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Actually,
if you use Stackdriver,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:10.980
it's a piece of cake.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:11.970
STEPHANIE WONG: What's that?

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:12.660
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Stackdriver is a tool

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.480
that lets you monitor and get
insight into your applications.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.110
STEPHANIE WONG: Sounds useful.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.080
How do we get started?

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.080
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Let me
jump right into a demo.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:25.730
OK.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.490
To start things off, we're going
to take an instance that I've

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.280
just set up here and
go ahead and install

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.040
Apache and PHP into it as
kind of a basic example.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.040
So I'll go ahead
and SSH into this.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.140
And this is just a
brand new small instance

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.422
I've set up that I haven't
done anything with yet.

00:00:41.422 --> 00:00:43.130
But we want to make
sure that we actually

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:44.810
have something to monitor.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.030
Of course, using
Stackdriver, you kind of

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:49.370
want to monitor and log
different events that

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:53.170
are going to come out of,
in this case, the default

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:54.200
Linux properties.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.010
But you can easily configure
that to add additional logging.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.380
We're just going to step
through the basics here.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:07.440
So I'll go ahead and
install Apache and PHP.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:08.690
Go ahead and let these update.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.815
And once those are done,
we'll make sure everything

00:01:10.815 --> 00:01:14.011
looks good, and then we'll
install the actual Stackdriver

00:01:14.011 --> 00:01:14.510
agents.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:19.980
So nothing special here.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.575
Let these run through.

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:30.700
OK, we'll skip that 30 seconds.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.566
And now that it's
all set up, we'll

00:01:32.566 --> 00:01:33.940
go ahead and make
sure everything

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:39.286
looks good by bringing
up this in a new tab.

00:01:39.286 --> 00:01:41.360
And we can see, there
we go, we have Apache2

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.552
successfully installed.

00:01:42.552 --> 00:01:44.260
So now what we're
going to do is actually

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.250
install the agent itself.

00:01:48.769 --> 00:01:51.060
We're going to go ahead and
look at a quick script that

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.640
basically just grabs
the different agents,

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.500
the monitoring agent
and the logging agent,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.640
and then installs them.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.660
So we'll go ahead and run those.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.035
And while that's
happening, we'll

00:02:02.035 --> 00:02:04.285
go ahead and take a look at
the Stackdriver interface.

00:02:04.285 --> 00:02:06.090
That will probably
take about a minute.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:07.950
So we know that Apache works.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.979
And in the Google
Cloud console, we

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:13.560
can take a look for Stackdriver.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.055
Now, if this is your first
time setting up Stackdriver

00:02:16.055 --> 00:02:18.180
or clicking on this monitoring
link which takes you

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.250
to the Stackdriver
interface, you

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.507
may have to go through
a small set up process.

00:02:22.507 --> 00:02:24.090
Basically, you're
just going to choose

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:28.320
what GCP project you're using
and some other small set

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.100
of steps.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.300
That can take another
couple of minutes as well.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.797
But once you have
that set up, you

00:02:32.797 --> 00:02:34.380
can easily just come
to this interface

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.450
and switch projects like you
might already be familiar with.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.650
So once we kind of have
those logs installed,

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.340
we should be able to go here to
resources, click on instances,

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:47.890
and we should see that the
instance is set up right here.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:50.430
You can see another instance
that I had set up earlier,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.780
but lamp instance is the one
we want to take a look at.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:54.370
So I'll click on that.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:57.820
And you can see just by kind
of running that quick command

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.680
line, we have the
agent installed,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.550
and it's already monitoring some
of the more standard metrics

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.350
that you might see
on an instance,

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.730
including CPU and memory
usage, network throughput,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.190
these kind of things.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.310
So this is a great way
to kind of just quickly

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:13.210
get that set up.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.790
One of the other great things
you can do in Stackdriver

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.890
is actually set up a dashboard.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:21.130
So if you go to Dashboards
and hit Create Dashboard,

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:22.990
you can start to add charts.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:24.790
Charts are basically
these metrics

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:27.760
that you're going to be able
to select from a list here.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.100
And kind of choosing
your instance,

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.530
get a better understanding of
creating a custom monitoring

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.950
dashboard for the metrics
that you actually care about.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.200
One of the other important
aspects of Stackdriver

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.030
is going to be alerting.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.790
So by setting up
an alerting policy,

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.340
we can easily take a
metric on an instance

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:50.012
and tell Stackdriver to notify
us when something happens.

00:03:50.012 --> 00:03:51.970
So if we go ahead and
add an example condition,

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:55.610
such as our instance here--

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.250
oh, we already have an instance.

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:00.220
We'll pick our lamp
instance, and we

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.160
can go ahead and choose
something like, if the CPU load

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.325
average is above 80%--

00:04:05.325 --> 00:04:07.450
that might be something
that we're concerned about,

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:10.240
because maybe that
means our web server is

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.000
serving too much traffic
or some process is locked.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.411
We'll go ahead and choose that.

00:04:14.411 --> 00:04:15.910
And then we'll add
the notification.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:18.220
So here we can add
an email, we can

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.070
add an SMS, a couple of
different integration options

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:21.640
here.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.350
There are tons of
things we can do.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:25.000
And then in
documentation, we can just

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.650
add some additional
texts in case

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:29.950
we want to set up some more
advanced monitoring or just

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:32.680
in case we want to
know what kind of flag

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.390
or what group this
was set to, we

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.100
can use that for custom text.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.674
Name the policy,
and then save it.

00:04:37.674 --> 00:04:40.090
And that way, we'll get notified
in case anything happens.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.500
Now, the next part we'll look
at is the actual logging.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.900
So if we come here
and click on logging,

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.880
we can see the default
logging interface which

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.590
is also using Stackdriver.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:54.979
On this dropdown here, we
can choose what instance

00:04:54.979 --> 00:04:55.770
we want to look at.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.320
In our case, we're going
to look at lamp instance.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.240
And in the subsequent
filter, we're

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.520
going to go ahead
and choose syslog.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.092
Once we've set
that to the filter,

00:05:04.092 --> 00:05:05.550
we can see all the
different things

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.300
that have been sent to syslog,
just so that we can measure

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.970
whatever applications
might be using or whatever

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.620
any other loggings--

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.320
well, anything that gets
sent to syslog, really.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.960
Just like that, you can set up
logging, monitoring, and more.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:19.370
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.660
Now I'll have a real grip on
my application in the cloud.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:22.120
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.120
If only it was that easy
to learn how to juggle.

00:05:25.989 --> 00:05:28.530
STEPHANIE WONG: Don't forget to
comment, like, and subscribe.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:31.030
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And if you
want to get in contact with us,

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.840
click on the link
in the description.

